WEBVTT

10.1.1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.725
In omp, a long-running checkout task crosses several information boundaries: what the model sees now, what persists later, and what integrations execute.

10.1.2
00:00:11.025 --> 00:00:21.500
Project instructions, always-carried rules, on-demand skills, and cross-session memory serve different lifetimes; choose the mechanism to match the information.

10.1.3
00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:30.425
Native project discovery stops at the nearest nonempty configuration directory, which can block instructions farther up the tree.

10.1.4
00:00:30.725 --> 00:00:38.675
Keep always-carried requirements short, and inspect the discovered sources before assuming the project instructions loaded.

10.2.1
00:00:39.775 --> 00:00:49.450
Time Traveling Stream Rules can interrupt a matching streamed attempt, inject corrective guidance, and retry; the facility is enabled by default.

10.2.2
00:00:49.750 --> 00:00:58.425
Regex rules can match during streaming, while AST rules inspect finalized source-bearing tool arguments before execution.

10.2.3
00:00:58.725 --> 00:01:06.625
Judged question rules wait for completed output, so they cannot interrupt the stream; their judge must also be available.

10.2.4
00:01:06.925 --> 00:01:12.725
Use this for corrective guidance, not as the security boundary around tool execution.

10.3.1
00:01:13.825 --> 00:01:23.600
As the checkout investigation grows, compaction reduces model-visible history to a summary and recent tail while preserving visible scrollback.

10.3.2
00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:32.600
Snapcompact instead renders older text into images locally, without a summarization model; the next model must support vision.

10.3.3
00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:40.175
That image archive is bounded and can be truncated, and reading it later still incurs the provider’s image costs.

10.3.4
00:01:40.475 --> 00:01:48.300
For a focused continuation, handoff generates a document within the same session rather than creating another agent.

10.4.1
00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:58.450
Cross-session memory is opt-in, with five documented modes including off: local summaries, Hindsight, Mnemopi, and Sharpshooter.

10.4.2
00:01:58.750 --> 00:02:07.750
Hindsight needs a reachable server; Mnemopi keeps local databases, but its default processing route can use an online model.

10.4.3
00:02:08.050 --> 00:02:17.950
Separate storage from processing: the local summary backend has no structured memory tools, and a local database does not imply offline inference.

10.4.4
00:02:18.250 --> 00:02:25.275
Treat a remembered coupon policy as a lead to verify against current source, not a permanent authority.

10.5.1
00:02:26.375 --> 00:02:34.800
Session history is a tree: return to an earlier checkout decision while ordinary branching preserves the alternative path.

10.5.2
00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:43.375
Tree navigation stays in the same session file; forking creates a new identity and, in persistent mode, a new file.

10.5.3
00:02:43.675 --> 00:02:52.325
Active context follows the selected ancestry; separate pruning and destructive rewrite operations can remove history.

10.6.1
00:02:53.425 --> 00:02:59.950
Plan mode gives the checkout change a review boundary, but fresh sessions do not enter it automatically.

10.6.2
00:03:00.250 --> 00:03:09.150
Prewalk is separate and off by default: it switches models after an eligible edit or write result, at the completed-turn boundary.

10.6.3
00:03:09.450 --> 00:03:14.475
The starting model may already have edited code before handing over the task.

10.7.1
00:03:15.575 --> 00:03:27.575
Skills supply instructions, tools execute functions, and extensions register capabilities or intercept lifecycle events; modern hook files use that extension runtime.

10.7.2
00:03:27.875 --> 00:03:36.150
MCP connects external tools, resources, and prompts, adding server-controlled instructions as another trust surface.

10.7.3
00:03:36.450 --> 00:03:43.350
Loading an executable extension means trusting its code, not simply adding a page of guidance.

10.8.1
00:03:44.450 --> 00:03:53.600
The documentation’s credential tables contain 79 distinct provider IDs, by our count, including local-engine rows.

10.8.2
00:03:53.900 --> 00:04:03.875
That describes table breadth, not independent model makers or usable accounts; Codex CLI also documents custom and local providers.

10.9.1
00:04:04.975 --> 00:04:15.025
Secret obfuscation is off by default and partial when enabled; static prompts, static tool definitions, and binaries fall outside its boundary.

10.9.2
00:04:15.325 --> 00:04:22.700
HTML exports can contain raw context, so review what a working session contains before sharing it.

10.9.3
00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:27.950
Choose deliberately what persists, what executes, and what can leave the machine.
